# mesa -- OpenGL, EGL and GBM.
#
# mutter renders through EGL and scans out through GBM, so this is what a GNOME
# session actually draws with. There is no fallback below it: gnome-shell
# refuses to start if it cannot create a GL context.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://mesa.freedesktop.org/archive/mesa-25.0.7.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=592272df3cf01e85e7db300c449df5061092574d099da275d19e97ef0510f8a6
SRC_DIR=mesa-25.0.7

# The driver list is chosen by architecture in build.sh, which is why there is
# no MESON_ARGS here.
#
# TWO THINGS THAT LOOK WRONG AFTER INSTALLING THIS AND ARE NOT.
#
# /usr/lib/dri is empty. Since mesa 24 there are no per-driver *_dri.so files
# at all: every gallium driver is linked into one libgallium-<version>.so and
# selected at runtime. Confirmed for this build -- libgallium-25.0.7.so
# contains llvmpipe, softpipe, virgl, panfrost, lima, etnaviv and freedreno on
# aarch64, and links libLLVM.so.20.1.
#
# There is no libGL.so. mesa is built with -Dglx=disabled because nothing here
# runs an X server, and libGL is the GLX entry point -- desktop OpenGL is
# reachable only through EGL. That is correct for this target, since mutter and
# GTK 4 render through EGL and GLES on Wayland, but an application that links
# -lGL directly will not run until Xwayland or libglvnd is packaged. A
# deliberate consequence of the Wayland-first decision, not an omission.
